    <h2>What Does EU Readiness Mean for Clothing Brands in 2025?</h2>
    <p>The EU is the world's most regulated market for clothing imports. Selling garments into Europe requires compliance with multiple overlapping regulations — and the landscape shifted significantly when <strong style="color:#fff;">GPSR became law in December 2024</strong>, replacing the older General Product Safety Directive. For brands sourcing from Bangladesh, the upside is significant: Bangladesh has 0% import duty to the EU under EBA, an established certification ecosystem, and factories increasingly oriented toward EU compliance requirements.</p>

    <h2>GPSR 2024 — What Changed for Clothing Brands</h2>
    <p>The General Product Safety Regulation (EU 2023/988) is the most significant change to EU product safety law in two decades. For clothing brands, the key new obligations are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Written risk assessments</strong> covering physical, chemical, and allergenic risks — maintained for 10 years per style</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">EU Responsible Person</strong> — non-EU brands must appoint an EU-based entity whose contact details appear on the product</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Product traceability identifiers</strong> — lot numbers, style codes — to enable targeted recalls without full market withdrawals</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Complaints register</strong> — documented internal process for safety complaints</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Many brands operating under the old GPSD are not yet GPSR-compliant. Amazon.de, Zalando, and other EU marketplaces are now enforcing GPSR documentation at supplier onboarding.</p>

    <h2>REACH Compliance for Garments — The Chemical Layer</h2>
    <p>REACH (EC 1907/2006) restricts hazardous substances in products sold in the EU. For Bangladesh-manufactured garments, the primary REACH risks are azo dyes in reactive-dyed fabrics, formaldehyde in crease-resistant finishes, chromium VI in leather patches, and cadmium or lead in metal trims. The most practical compliance route is <strong style="color:#fff;">OEKO-TEX Standard 100 certification</strong> — which tests every fabric component against REACH restrictions and produces documentation accepted by EU customs and retailers. SDF Clothing uses OEKO-TEX certified fabrics across standard ranges.</p>

    <h2>EU Textile Labelling — The Most Commonly Missed Requirement</h2>
    <p>EU Regulation 1007/2011 is frequently violated by brands new to European markets. The most common mistakes are using invented fabric names instead of official EU fibre names, English-only labels when selling in Germany, France, or Italy, and missing the animal origin declaration on products with leather patches or down filling. SDF Clothing's team can produce compliant multilingual labels as part of production.</p>

    <h2>ESPR and the Digital Product Passport — Prepare Now</h2>
    <p>The EU's Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation introduces the <strong style="color:#fff;">Digital Product Passport</strong> — mandatory QR-accessible data on material composition, origin, chemical compliance, and recyclability. Textile enforcement begins mid-2028, but brands collecting supply chain data now — fabric mill, fibre certification, chemical test results — will have a substantial compliance and commercial advantage when delegated acts are finalised in 2027.</p>

    <h2>Bangladesh EBA Duty-Free — How to Claim It Correctly</h2>
    <p>Bangladesh garments enter the EU at <strong style="color:#fff;">0% import duty</strong> under EBA — compared to 12% from China. To claim this, garments must meet the "double transformation" Rules of Origin (fabric woven in Bangladesh, garment cut and sewn in Bangladesh), and the factory must provide a GSP Form A certificate or REX statement of origin with every shipment. SDF Clothing provides full EBA origin documentation as standard.</p>

